# Summary
Introduced an information disclosure: DB errors now expose the rusqlite source text in client-visible error responses.
The commit changes StoreError::Db and MetadataError::Db to include the underlying rusqlite::Error in their Display output. HTTP error responses embed err.to_string() directly into the XML body, so any database failure now leaks internal error strings (e.g., file paths, schema/constraint names) to authenticated clients. This increases information disclosure compared to the prior generic message.

Update: fixed in commit `b0dab49` (`Sanitize internal error responses`).
The fix was applied in the HTTP error translation layer rather than the storage
error types: detailed `StoreError`, `MetadataError`, `Ec`, `IntegrityError`,
`MetadataBlobError`, and `InternalError` strings are still preserved internally
for debugging/logging, but `S3Response::error` now maps those `InternalError`-class
responses to a generic client-visible message:
`We encountered an internal error. Please try again.`
Regression tests were added to ensure internal details are not exposed in the
XML `<Message>` field for `StoreError`, `InternalError { reason }`, and
`MetadataBlobError`.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm StoreError::Db / MetadataError::Db include rusqlite::Error in Display output (storage error.rs).
- [x] Confirm ServerError wraps those errors and preserves their Display text (server error.rs).
- [x] Confirm HTTP error responses embed err.to_string() in XML <Message> (response.rs + xml.rs).
- [x] Identify DB contexts that surface internal details (bucket_db.rs open/init contexts).
- [ ] Reproduce an HTTP response containing a rusqlite error at runtime (blocked by build/dependency constraints).
## Report
Dynamic attempts were blocked by environment constraints: `cargo build -p server` failed due to crates.io access being blocked (tiny_http download 403), so no debug binary could be produced for a crash run. `cargo build -p storage --offline` also failed during workspace resolution because tiny_http was unavailable. Valgrind is not installed (`valgrind --version` -> command not found). LLDB is present but could not run since `target/debug/argmin-s3` does not exist (lldb error: target does not exist). 

Code-level validation shows the disclosure: `StoreError::Db` and `MetadataError::Db` include `{source}` in their Display strings (crates/storage/src/error.rs:24-29, 47-52), which means the rusqlite error text is appended to the message. `ServerError` wraps these errors with Display formatting (`storage error: {0}` / `metadata error: {0}`; crates/server/src/error.rs:18-22), and HTTP responses embed `err.to_string()` into XML via `Response::error` (crates/server/src/http/response.rs:272-280) and `xml::error_xml` places that message in the `<Message>` field (crates/server/src/http/xml.rs:7-18). DB operations add context strings such as "open bucket database" (crates/storage/src/bucket_db.rs:21-30), so a failing DB open will yield a message like `database error: open bucket database: <rusqlite error>`, which is then returned to authenticated clients in XML. This confirms the information disclosure risk introduced by the commit.

